.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt DEADMAN.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm deadman.conf
.Nd checks for deadman
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
lists the checks that
.Xr deadman 8
watches, one to a line, each in five fields separated by blanks:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar name
What the check is called in mail and status output: up to 64 letters,
digits, dots, underscores and hyphens, not starting with a dot or a
hyphen.
.It Ar token
The last part of the check's ping URL: 32 lowercase hexadecimal digits,
which
.Ql deadman token
makes.
Each check needs its own.
.It Ar period
How often the check should ping: a whole number followed by
.Ql s ,
.Ql m ,
.Ql h ,
.Ql d
or
.Ql w ,
for seconds, minutes, hours, days or weeks.
.It Ar grace
How much longer to wait, once the period has passed, before calling the
check down, in the same form.
It is also how long a run that has pinged
.Pa start
has to finish.
.It Ar mailto
Where to send mail about the check: an address, a local user, or several
of either separated by commas, without blanks.
.El
.Pp
A
.Ql #
begins a comment, which runs to the end of the line.
Blank lines are ignored.
.Pp
A token is the only thing a ping URL needs, so the file should belong to
root and be mode 0600;
.Xr deadman 8
reads it before giving up root.
To revoke a token, change or remove its line: on its next run,
.Xr deadman 8
removes the old token's ping files, and its URL stops working.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/deadman.conf" -compact
.It Pa /etc/deadman.conf
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A nightly backup that pings when it finishes, given a day's grace, and a
mail queue check that pings every five minutes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# name		token				period	grace	mailto
backups		6b1e0c3f9a7d42e8b5c1d0f4a2e9c7b3	1d	1d	root
mailq		0f4a2e9c7b36b1e0c3f9a7d42e8b5c1d	5m	10m	ops@example.org
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr deadman 8 ,
.Xr deadman.cgi 8
